now a russian figure skater uh camilla  camila valeva will be allowed to compete  at the beijing olympics following a  decision by the court of arbitration for  sport the 15 year old helped the  russians win the team event last week  but then reports of a positive doping  test for a heart medication from  december emerged  the court ruled that valeeva should not  be provisionally suspended ahead of a  full hearing into the case she's favored  to win gold at the women's individual  event which takes place tomorrow  let's get more from dw sports  correspondent jonathan crane who's in  beijing welcome jonathan so just first  of all explain the court's ruling to us  well essentially the court had to  balance two things one is the integrity  of competition if it turns out valia is  guilty of doping how would it damage  that integrity but the other thing  that's had to weigh up is the fairness  on valeeva she is 15 years old as such  she's a protected person under  anti-doping rules and that means that  she is entitled to less harsher um  punishments and essentially the court  said look if valeeva wasn't allowed to  compete and it turns out later that she  isn't guilty of doping then that would  cause her personally significant harm  now  the court also said it was unacceptable  that it took six weeks for this positive  doping test to come to light that  clearly is not valeeva's fault it's not  her fault that this has overshadowed her  olympic games now we had a press  conference uh today from the court of  arbitration for sport there were no  questions but this is what the general  secretary matia reb said in a statement  the panel considered fundamental  principles of fairness  irreparable harm and the relative  balance of interest as between the  applicants and the athlete who did not  test positive  during the olympic games in beijing  but is still subject to a disciplinary  procedure on the merits following her  positive anti-doping test  undergone in december 2021 in particular  the panel considered that preventing the  athlete to compete at the olympic games  would cause her irreparable harm  in these circumstances  so jonathan crane this is not uh done  and dusted there are still lots of  uncertainties  yeah indeed while we're going to see  valeeva compete now and there's no  doubting she's an incredible talent she  was the first woman to land a quadruple  jumping competition not just once but  twice  the court hasn't ruled whether she is  guilty of doping or not that will  probably come at a later stage because  valeeva is entitled to have her b sample  tested that is effectively a backup  sample that would confirm whether the  first test was indeed positive now the  ioc wanted everything done and dusted  now they wanted that to be heard as well  but uh one of the parties didn't agree  we can only assume that was the russian  side so it may well be that valeava  comes away from beijing with two gold  medals to her name she is the heavy  favorite to win gold in the individual  event and yet be stripped of those gold  medals at a later date  and as you mentioned um uh camilla  valeeva is only 15 so what does this  controversy tell us about the adults  advising her  yes of course i mean we said again we  mustn't forget that she is 15 years old  so  even if she did dope there are question  marks about whether she would be solely  responsible for that clearly she puts a  lot of trust in the people around her be  it her coaches team advisors doctors now  wada the world anti-doping agency has  said it will investigate her entourage  there are kind of echoes here aren't  there of of east germany and the doping  regime there where young athletes were  given banned substances dressed up as  vitamins now whatever happens again  russian doping has come into the  spotlight the whole reason one of the  reasons russia's ban was cut was to  protect a younger generation of athletes  and allow them to compete so there is a  certain irony in the fact that it is a  young athlete now at the center of  another doping controversy it's a very  messy situation all round  thank that jonathan jonathan crane in  beijing
 

